    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d60">

                <pb facs="0245" n="214" xml:id="pg214"/>

                <head>60. Memorandum From the President’s Assistant for National Security Affairs
                    (McFarlane) to President Reagan<anchor n="1" xml:id="d60-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date
                            ana="#date_undated-inferred-from-sibling-dates" calendar="gregorian"
                            notAfter="1985-07-17T21:49:00+00:00"
                            notBefore="1985-07-15T00:00:00-04:00">undated</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>SUBJECT</label>
                    <item>Paper on the Soviet Union</item>
                </list>
                <p>As background information while you prepare for your meeting with Gorbachev, I
                    have asked my staff to prepare a series of papers dealing with basic aspects of
                    the Soviet system and typical Soviet attitudes.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d60-f2"
                    /></p>
                <p>Attached at Tab A is the first of these papers, which discusses the fundamental
                    question of whether the Soviet Union is an ideological power or simply an
                    expansionist power in the traditional Russian mold. I think you will find it
                    interesting reading.</p>


                <xi:include href="d60-a1.xml" xpointer="d60-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d60-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d60-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Reagan Library, Jack Matlock Files, Chronological File, 1980–1986,
                    Matlock Chron July 1985 (3/8). Confidential. Sent for information. Drafted by
                    Matlock. The memorandum is unsigned.</p>
            </note>
